WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.992
Let's walk through this proof: Sum of first n integers.

00:00:03.992 --> 00:00:07.405
The claim: the sum of the first n integers.

00:00:07.405 --> 00:00:10.060
This is the claim to be proved.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.680
Base case: n = 1 works.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.660
Assume the formula holds for n.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:21.224
Add the (n+1)-th term to the sum.

00:00:21.224 --> 00:00:27.470
Adding the next term (n+1) to both sides of the inductive hypothesis.

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:33.275
Simplify to the formula for n+1.

00:00:33.275 --> 00:00:43.684
Algebraic simplification: factor out (n+1) to get (n+1)[n/2 + 1] = (n+1)[(n+2)/2].

00:00:43.684 --> 00:00:49.756
Notice that a step is skipped here.

00:00:49.756 --> 00:00:58.430
Factor out (n+1): (n+1)(n/2 + 1) = (n+1)(n+2/2) = (n+1)(n+2)/2.

00:00:59.030 --> 00:00:59.935
Induction complete!

00:00:59.935 --> 00:01:04.008
So, in summary: The proof is logically sound, but

00:01:04.008 --> 00:01:08.080
S5 skips the algebraic factoring step required to show

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.700
how the terms combine into the target formula.
